# Feature: reaction-center symmetry on a local-function rate law — a
#          SYMMETRIC bimolecular rule (both reactants the same molecule type,
#          binding on the same component) with one reactant tagged.
# Tests:   that BNG2's symmetry_factor reaches the local-rate propensity
#          paths.  BNG2 emits symmetry_factor="0.5" for this rule; RM's
#          FunctionProduct / local-function propensity is built directly from
#          Σ w·f(mol) and used to skip the factor entirely, so the rule ran
#          at exactly 2x its intended rate.
#
#          `compute_propensity` (every other rate law) has always applied it,
#          and no other model in any of the three corpora pairs a non-unit
#          symmetry_factor with a local function — this model is the only
#          coverage of that combination.
#
# Oracle:  BNG2 network -> ODE.  Listed in NFSIM_UNRELIABLE: the pinned
#          NFsim release has this same defect and reads 2x fast here.  It is
#          an NFsim bug, not a semantic disagreement — stock NFsim
#          constructs every rate law except the setBaseRate() ones with
#          baseRate=1 and never recovers the factor.  Fixed upstream in
#          bngsim's vendored NFsim (lanl/bngsim#195, fixed in #278); the
#          patch comment names "global function, local function (DOR),
#          function product, MM" as the affected set.
#
#          Measured, Afree at t = 10/20/30:
#            BNG2 ODE          60.8 / 33.8 / 23.6   <- oracle
#            RM (fixed)        61.0 / 33.7 / 24.2
#            RM (before)       33.7 / 18.9 / 12.8   <- 2x fast
#            NFsim 2.9.3       33.5 / 17.9 / 13.0   <- same defect
#
# Tier:    base.
# Expected: A(b) is consumed in pairs; m~1 molecules are twice as likely to
#          be drawn into the tagged slot as m~0 ones, so Afree collapses fast
#          and Amod stays pinned (the rule never changes m).
# Note:    a symmetric rule with a tagged reactant also routes through the
#          same-molecule null-event rejection rather than the
#          `same_components` ab_both deflation.  That path yields the
#          *ordered* distinct-pair sum, which is what the 0.5 then converts
#          to unordered — the two corrections compose rather than collide.
#          Untagged homodimer rules are unaffected and keep the implicit /2
#          in compute_propensity (tests/cpp/homodimer_rate_test.cpp pins that
#          against the chemical master equation).
# invariant: conserved Atot = 400
# invariant: conserved Amod = 200

begin model
begin parameters
    kc   1.0e-3
end parameters

begin molecule types
    A(b,m~0~1)
end molecule types

begin seed species
    A(b,m~0)   200
    A(b,m~1)   200
end seed species

begin observables
    Molecules  Afree   A(b)
    Molecules  Amod    A(m~1)
    Molecules  Atot    A()
    Molecules  Dim     A(b!1).A(b!1)
end observables

begin functions
    # 1 for an m~0 molecule, 2 for an m~1 one (molecule-level tag scope).
    lf(x) = kc*(1+Amod(x))
end functions

begin reaction rules
    R: A(b)%x + A(b) -> A(b!1)%x.A(b!1)   lf(x)
end reaction rules
end model

begin actions
simulate({method=>"nf",t_end=>30,n_steps=>60})
end actions
